---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro'
import { hospital } from '../data/site'
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Grievance Redressal"
  subtitle="How to raise a complaint or concern, our service timelines and the escalation matrix."
  updated="September 2026"
>
  <h2>Our Commitment</h2>
  <p>
    At {hospital.name}, every patient's experience matters. If something falls short of your expectations, we want to hear
    about it so we can put it right and improve. This page explains how to raise a grievance, what to expect from us, and
    how to escalate a matter if it is not resolved to your satisfaction.
  </p>

  <h2>Grievance Officer</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Grievance Officer — {hospital.name}</strong><br />
    {hospital.address.line1}, {hospital.address.line2}, {hospital.address.city}, {hospital.address.state} — {hospital.address.pincode}<br />
    Email: {hospital.email}<br />
    Phone: {hospital.phones.reception}<br />
    Working hours: Monday to Saturday, 10:00 AM – 4:00 PM (excluding public holidays)
  </p>

  <h2>How to Raise a Grievance</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Speak to the team first.</strong> Most concerns can be resolved quickly by the nursing station, the treating doctor, the department in-charge, or the front desk.</li>
    <li><strong>Contact the Grievance Officer.</strong> If the matter is not resolved, submit a written or emailed complaint to the Grievance Officer using the contact details above, or use the <a href="/contact">contact form</a> and mark the topic as a complaint.</li>
    <li><strong>Provide details.</strong> Please include your name, contact number, date and time of the incident, the department involved, and a short description of what happened. Do not include unrelated sensitive medical details in email.</li>
    <li><strong>Receive an acknowledgement.</strong> We will acknowledge your complaint within <strong>3 working days</strong> with a reference number.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Service Timelines</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Acknowledgement:</strong> within 3 working days of receipt.</li>
    <li><strong>Investigation and response:</strong> within <strong>15 working days</strong> for most complaints.</li>
    <li><strong>Complex or clinical complaints</strong> requiring case review may take up to <strong>30 working days</strong>; you will be kept informed of the reason for any extension.</li>
    <li><strong>Emergency or patient-safety concerns</strong> are escalated immediately to the Medical Superintendent / Director.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Escalation Matrix</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Level</th><th>Who to contact</th><th>Timeframe</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Level 1</td><td>Department in-charge / Nursing head / Front desk</td><td>Immediate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Level 2</td><td>Grievance Officer</td><td>Within 3 working days acknowledgement, 15 working days resolution</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Level 3</td><td>Medical Superintendent / Hospital Director</td><td>If unresolved after Level 2, or within 7 working days for serious safety concerns</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Level 4</td><td>National / State authorities (see below)</td><td>As per the applicable authority's process</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>External Escalation</h2>
  <p>Depending on the nature of the complaint, you may also approach:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>National Medical Commission (NMC)</strong> or the relevant State Medical Council — for professional-conduct complaints against a registered medical practitioner.</li>
    <li><strong>National / State Health Authority</strong> — for complaints under the Clinical Establishments Act, where applicable.</li>
    <li><strong>Consumer Commission</strong> — for consumer disputes regarding services and charges.</li>
    <li><strong>Cyber Crime Helpline 1930</strong> or local police — where fraud or criminal conduct is suspected.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What We Ask of You</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Please raise your concern as early as possible while details are fresh.</li>
    <li>Stay factual and courteous; abusive or violent conduct towards staff is not acceptable and may be reported.</li>
    <li>Do not share personal health information of others, and remove any unrelated personal data from written complaints.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Review and Improvement</h2>
  <p>
    All complaints, their root causes and corrective actions are reviewed by hospital leadership at regular quality
    meetings, in line with NABH quality-improvement requirements. Your feedback directly helps us improve patient safety
    and experience.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
